<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.12.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01956</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.12.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>4a70698b-7014-423e-b922-6685ecffe4ea</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="06f9bcda-dafd-43fb-b8d6-423f240a460c" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--q1-->
  <exercise data-content-id="d21eb73e-0532-407c-8c17-041efa2bbf99" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\(f(x)=x^2-3\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(f(x)=x^2+5\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=x^2-3\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=x^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=x^2+7\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Choose the quadratic equation that is represented by the graph.</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/bd59d8abb03c9dac56adad4cf8fd4dc0d9bf2f64"/></media></figure></para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The graph represents \(f(x)=x^2-3\) because the \(y\)-intercept is \((0,-3)\) and the coefficient of the term \(x^2\) is a positive 1, so the graph should open upward.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.5. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q2-->
  <exercise data-content-id="6e27480e-e352-49be-9b6f-44f63467d9a5" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\(f(x)=x^2\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(f(x)=x^2+5\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=x^2-3\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=x^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=x^2+7\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Choose the quadratic equation that is represented by the graph.</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/adfa84a6542f9a442bd0985740fca99dde05c3fe"/></media></figure></para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The graph represents \(f(x)=x^2\) because the \(y\)-intercept is \((0,0)\) and the coefficient of the term \(x^2\) is a positive 1, so the graph should open upward.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.5. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q3-->
  <exercise data-content-id="bfabbcb1-2b5d-4d2e-8a10-460822d6bb93" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\(f(x)=x^2+7\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(f(x)=x^2+5\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=x^2-3\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=x^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=x^2+7\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Choose the quadratic equation that is represented by the graph.</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/db8c1a97b5b5d84b10453c8245ae8f09992c8ff9"/></media></figure></para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The graph represents \(f(x)=x^2+7\) because the \(y\)-intercept is \((0,7)\) and the coefficient of the term \(f(x)=x^2+7\) is a positive 1, so the graph should open upward.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.5. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q4-->
  <exercise data-content-id="ba4aa0c4-e1a5-4e68-874d-f6737f811d7f" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\(f(x)=x^2+5\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(f(x)=x^2+5\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=x^2-3\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=x^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=x^2+7\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Choose the quadratic equation that is represented by the graph.</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/cd72afad226904dbabcd17ff4213295c0c300836"/></media></figure></para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The graph represents \(f(x)=x^2+5\) because the \(y\)-intercept is \((0,5)\) and the coefficient of the term \(f(x)=x^2+5\) is a positive 1, so the graph should open upward.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.5. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q5a-->
  <exercise data-content-id="94861d4e-d17e-4df1-9d09-0e8774e8e2c6" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="factored form" data-solution-options="[&quot;factored form&quot;, &quot;standard form&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>The following two equations are equivalent.</para>
      <para>factored form \(f(x)=(x+2)(x+3)\) 
        standard form \(f(x)=x^2+5x+6\) </para>
      <para> Which equation helps find the \(x\)-intercepts most efficiently?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The equation written in factored form \(f(x)=(x+2)(x+3)\) because the factors in the expression allow us to see when the expression is 0.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.4.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--5b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="e021a697-88ee-4d81-a205-df68f64b7376" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="standard form" data-solution-options="[&quot;factored form&quot;, &quot;standard form&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> Which equation helps find the \(y\)-intercept most efficiently?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The equation written in standard form \(f(x)=x^2+5x+6\) because the constant term shows us the \(y\)-intercept.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.4.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--6a-->
  <exercise data-content-id="549853a5-000e-484f-90c1-79f2bae27211" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="Blue graph (in the middle, opening upward)" data-solution-options="[&quot;Green graph (at the bottom, opening downward)&quot;, &quot;Blue graph (in the middle, opening upward)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> There are three equations represented on this graph:</para>
      <para>\(f(x)=x^2\) (black graph, at the top, opening upward)
        \(f(x)=x^2-4\)
        \(f(x)=-x^2+5\)</para>
      <para>Use the graph to answer questions a and b.</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/f02a214586d1dccfe80f82e0a2c83c4656637b78"/></media></figure></para>
      <para> Which graph represents the equation \(f(x)=x^2-4\)?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The blue graph represents \(f(x)=x^2-4\) because the \(y\)-intercept is \((0,-4)\) and the coefficient of the term \(x^2\) is a positive 1, so the graph should open upward. </para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.12.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--6b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="10205e3d-e739-41b1-8b9e-175685ffe51b" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="Green graph (at the bottom, opening downward)" data-solution-options="[&quot;Green graph (at the bottom, opening downward)&quot;, &quot;Blue graph (in the middle, opening upward)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> Which graph represents the equation \(f(x)=-x^2+5\)?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The green graph represents \(f(x)=-x^2+5\) because the \(y\)-intercept is \((0,5)\) and the coefficient of the term \(x^2\) is a negative 1, so the graph should open downward. </para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.12.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q7-->
  <exercise data-content-id="4c5dd1eb-ea08-432e-bf96-40ef5667ac83" data-problem-type="multiselect" data-solution="[&quot;\\(f(x)=(x-1)^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=(x+1)(x+2)\\)&quot;]" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(f(x)=x^2+3x-2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=x^2-10x\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=(x-1)^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=5x^2-3x-5\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=(x+1)(x+2)\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> Select two equations whose graphs have a positive \(y\)-intercept.</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \(f(x)=(x-1)^2\), \(f(x)=(x+1)(x+2)\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.12.2.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q8-->
  <exercise data-content-id="5b8cb81c-00b1-4688-b76b-3c1b4ab07a79" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\(f(x)=(x-5)^2\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(f(x)=x^2+5\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=x^2-5\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=(x+5)^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=(x-5)^2\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Which function represents the graph of \(f(x)=x^2\) shifted 5 units to the right?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The function is \(f(x)=(x-5)^2\) because subtracting 5 from \(x\) shifts the graph to the right 5 units.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.5. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q9-->
  <exercise data-content-id="0abd8816-8058-492b-ba67-a32d84901a2c" data-problem-type="multiselect" data-solution="[&quot;\\(f(x)=1-x^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=-(x-1)^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=(1-x)(x+2)\\)&quot;]" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(f(x)=(1-x)^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=1-x^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=-(x-1)^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=-1+x^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=(1-x)(x+2)\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para><emphasis>Select three</emphasis> equations whose graphs open downward.</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \(f(x)=1-x^2\), \(f(x)=-(x-1)^2\), \(f(x)=(1-x)(x+2)\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.12.2 and activity 7.12.5.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q10-->
  <exercise data-content-id="40de6698-d5bb-4a0c-9372-f40f8f6b0a44" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\(f(x)=(3x)^2\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(f(x)=3x^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=(3x)^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=\\frac13x^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)={(\\frac13x)}^2\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Which curve is the narrowest?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The function is \(f(x)=(3x)^2\) is the narrowest because multiplying x by a value greater than 1 makes the curve grow faster. \((3x)^2 =9x^2\) , so \(f(x)=(3x)^2\) is the narrowest curve.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.5. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q11-->
  <exercise data-content-id="59bc177a-332c-4bc1-b521-4f21086e378d" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\(f(x)=-{(x-4)}^2+4\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(f(x)={(x-4)}^2+4\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)={(x+4)}^2+4\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=-{(x-4)}^2+4\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=-{(x+4)}^2+4\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Which equation is represented by the graph?</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/fc657952ff3dc0167c3490f2019580516f49e586"/></media></figure></para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The graph represents \(f(x)=-{(x-4)}^2+4\) because the graph is moved 4 units right and 4 units up and the squared term is multiplied by negative 1, so the graph should open downward. </para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.5.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q12-->
  <exercise data-content-id="46ffb136-295e-46ce-9789-3497e2b34928" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\(f(x)={(x+2)}^2+3\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(f(x)={(x-2)}^2+3\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)={(x-3)}^2+2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)={(x+2)}^2+3\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)={(x+3)}^2+2\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Which equation is represented by the graph?</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="Graph on coordinate plane. Horizontal axis by twos from negative 8 to 8. Vertical axis by twos from negative 4 to 8. Graph goes through point 0 comma 7."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/0c9e5c47508f4c799bd5a6d9399b43a536189bb2"/></media></figure></para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The graph represents \(f(x)=(x+2)^2+3\) because the graph is moved 2 units left and 3 units up and the squared term is multiplied by positive 1, so the graph should open upward. </para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.5. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q#13-->
  <exercise data-content-id="1ce92a00-0789-432e-9eee-cadf08545987" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-solution="Graph d" data-solution-options="[&quot;Graph b&quot;, &quot;Graph a&quot;, &quot;Graph c&quot;, &quot;Graph d&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>For questions 13 - 14, use the following graph. </para>
      <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/07e59d96075a1ea4852d468146010b7fdf6385e8"/></media></figure></para>
      <para>Which of the graphs has been horizontally stretched? </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! Graph d</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Try Again. Revisit activity 7.12.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q#14-->
  <exercise data-content-id="0a7c4d1b-b296-460c-b1de-e2e27b90f8d1" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-solution="\(x+9=52\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(f(x) = (x - 2.5)^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x) = (4x)^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x) = (x + 2.5)^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x) = (\\frac14x)^2\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Which function most closely represents the equation for graph c?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! <emphasis/></para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Try Again. Revisit activity 7.12.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
